WEBVTT

00:00.540 --> 00:02.305
- [Voiceover] So we've
got the function g here,

00:02.305 --> 00:04.789
and what I want you to
do is pause the video

00:04.789 --> 00:06.763
and figure out what g of one is,

00:06.763 --> 00:08.968
figure out what g of two is,

00:08.968 --> 00:13.334
g of three, and g of four.

00:13.334 --> 00:16.143
Figure out what all four of these are.

00:16.143 --> 00:18.303
All right, now let's work
through this together.

00:18.303 --> 00:22.134
So g of one, if n is equal to one,

00:22.134 --> 00:23.922
well, then we're gonna hit
this case right over here,

00:23.922 --> 00:27.242
if n is equal to one, g is equal to four.

00:27.242 --> 00:29.332
So that was pretty straightforward.

00:29.332 --> 00:31.816
Now, g of two,

00:31.816 --> 00:33.837
if n is equal to two,

00:33.837 --> 00:36.902
well, two is greater than
one and it's a whole number,

00:36.902 --> 00:38.828
and so we would use this case.

00:38.828 --> 00:39.688
And this is interesting,

00:39.688 --> 00:41.847
because it's defined in
terms of the function,

00:41.847 --> 00:43.728
but it's not defined in terms of g of n,

00:43.728 --> 00:45.864
but g of n minus one.

00:45.864 --> 00:48.859
So if n is two, 'cause we're
evaluating g of two here,

00:48.859 --> 00:51.715
this is going to be g of two minus one,

00:51.715 --> 00:55.845
or g of one, plus 3.2.

00:55.845 --> 00:58.215
Plus 3.2.

00:58.215 --> 00:59.793
Well, what's that going to be?

00:59.793 --> 01:02.695
Well, g of one, we know is equal to four.

01:02.695 --> 01:04.298
We just figured that out.

01:04.298 --> 01:08.969
So four plus 3.2, that is 7.2.

01:09.359 --> 01:10.690
All right, let's keep going.

01:10.690 --> 01:13.472
G of three, we're gonna
fall into this case again,

01:13.472 --> 01:16.266
because three is greater than
one and it's a whole number.

01:16.266 --> 01:20.005
So this is going to be
g of three minus one,

01:20.005 --> 01:23.451
or g of two, plus 3.2.

01:23.451 --> 01:24.721
Plus 3.2.

01:24.721 --> 01:27.794
Well, we know what g of two is. It's 7.2.

01:27.794 --> 01:30.964
We just figured that out. It is 7.2.

01:30.964 --> 01:34.083
7.2 plus 3.2 is going

01:34.083 --> 01:37.183
to be equal to 10.4.

01:37.183 --> 01:40.321
And then g of four,
well, we fall here again.

01:40.321 --> 01:43.041
This is gonna be g of three plus 3.2.

01:43.041 --> 01:47.174
G of three plus 3.2.

01:47.174 --> 01:49.014
What is that going to be equal to?

01:49.014 --> 01:51.700
Well, g of three we just
figured out is 10.4.

01:51.700 --> 01:54.392
10.4 plus 3.2 is going

01:54.392 --> 01:57.352
to be 13.6.

01:57.352 --> 01:58.456
And so what you have here,

01:58.456 --> 01:59.942
this is actually quite interesting.

01:59.942 --> 02:03.449
You can think of this function g,

02:03.449 --> 02:06.955
and we see that it's defined
over all positive integers.

02:06.955 --> 02:08.766
Because it's defined
over positive integers,

02:08.766 --> 02:11.041
we could think of it
as defining a sequence,

02:11.041 --> 02:12.598
and we see what the sequence here is.

02:12.598 --> 02:16.335
The first term is four,

02:16.335 --> 02:19.625
second term is 7.2,

02:19.625 --> 02:23.035
next term is 10.4,

02:23.035 --> 02:25.965
next term is 13.6,

02:25.965 --> 02:27.968
and it could keep going on and on and on.

02:27.968 --> 02:30.986
And what's happening? What's
happening in this sequence?

02:30.986 --> 02:32.867
Well, we're starting with four.

02:32.867 --> 02:34.121
We're starting with four,

02:34.121 --> 02:36.629
and this case of the
function gave us that.

02:36.629 --> 02:38.436
If n is equal to four,

02:38.436 --> 02:40.204
if n is equal to one,

02:40.204 --> 02:42.086
the function is going to be equal to four,

02:42.086 --> 02:44.524
and then for each term after that,

02:44.524 --> 02:47.425
you take the previous
term and you add 3.2.

02:47.425 --> 02:51.419
So we add 3.2 for the
second term, we add 3.2,

02:51.419 --> 02:53.473
so we just keep adding,

02:53.473 --> 02:58.293
we just keep adding 3.2,

02:58.293 --> 03:02.982
not .32, 3.2 to get to the next term.

03:02.982 --> 03:04.676
Now, we could've defined it
that way, we could've said,

03:04.676 --> 03:06.546
"Hey, let's have a sequence
where the first term is four

03:06.546 --> 03:10.120
"and then we keep adding
3.2 to get each next term."

03:10.120 --> 03:12.781
But this is another
interesting way of defining it.

03:12.781 --> 03:14.243
And this way of defining it,

03:14.243 --> 03:16.589
where we defined it as
an algebraic function,

03:16.589 --> 03:19.793
a function that's defined
over all positive integers,

03:19.793 --> 03:21.302
where we have a base case.

03:21.302 --> 03:22.745
And a base case, really, in this case,

03:22.745 --> 03:24.483
gave us our first term,

03:24.483 --> 03:25.876
and then we have this other case

03:25.876 --> 03:28.082
that's defined in terms of the function.

03:28.082 --> 03:30.079
Then you have to recurse backwards

03:30.079 --> 03:32.099
to eventually get to a base case.

03:32.099 --> 03:34.816
We call this a recursive function.

03:34.816 --> 03:38.786
Recursive function.

03:38.786 --> 03:40.366
So with this example, we're seeing

03:40.366 --> 03:42.827
how a recursive function can be used

03:42.827 --> 03:45.636
to define an actual sequence.

03:45.636 --> 03:47.656
And we went in order here,

03:47.656 --> 03:48.910
but you could've gone
the other way around.

03:48.910 --> 03:52.499
If I said, "Oh, well, what's g of,

03:52.499 --> 03:54.629
"what's g of six?"

03:54.629 --> 03:55.945
Well, you'd go into this case, you'd say,

03:55.945 --> 04:00.008
"OK, that's going to
be g of five plus 3.2."

04:00.008 --> 04:03.398
It's gonna be the previous term plus 3.2

04:03.398 --> 04:05.650
if we view it as a sequence.

04:05.650 --> 04:06.533
Well, then we're gonna have to figure out

04:06.533 --> 04:07.438
what the previous term is.

04:07.438 --> 04:11.138
G of five is going to be g of four,

04:11.138 --> 04:14.278
g of four plus 3.2,

04:14.278 --> 04:16.262
and you would keep going
back and back and back,

04:16.262 --> 04:18.119
but we've already figured
out what g of four is.

04:18.119 --> 04:21.487
It's 13.6,

04:21.487 --> 04:25.357
so this is 16.8,

04:25.357 --> 04:29.496
and then if g of five is 16.8,

04:29.496 --> 04:33.095
16.8, you add 3.2 there, you would get 20.

04:33.095 --> 04:34.980
So you could start at g of
six and keep backing up,

04:34.980 --> 04:36.350
all the way until you get to g of one,

04:36.350 --> 04:37.719
and then you figure out what that is.

04:37.719 --> 04:40.274
You recurse back to your base case,

04:40.274 --> 04:43.292
and then you're able to
fill in all of the blanks.

04:43.292 --> 04:45.241
Let's do a few more examples of this.

04:46.311 --> 04:48.191
So we have this function here.

04:48.191 --> 04:50.606
So let's say that this defines a sequence.

04:50.606 --> 04:52.603
Let's think about what
the first four terms

04:52.603 --> 04:54.089
of that sequence are, and once again,

04:54.089 --> 04:56.389
I encourage you to pause the
video and figure that out.

04:57.409 --> 04:59.453
All right, let's work through it.

04:59.453 --> 05:03.562
So h of one is, well,
they very clearly tell us

05:03.562 --> 05:04.747
that's going to be 14.

05:04.747 --> 05:07.811
If n is equal to one, h is 14.

05:07.811 --> 05:11.172
H of two,

05:11.172 --> 05:13.242
well, now we're falling into this case,

05:13.242 --> 05:15.659
'cause two is greater than
one and it's a whole number,

05:15.659 --> 05:19.336
so it's gonna be 28 over h of one,

05:19.336 --> 05:21.246
over h of one.

05:21.246 --> 05:22.973
Well, we know h of one is 14,

05:22.973 --> 05:26.738
so it's gonna be 28 over
14, which is equal to two.

05:26.738 --> 05:29.572
Now h of three.

05:29.572 --> 05:32.358
H of three, we're gonna
fall into this case again.

05:32.358 --> 05:34.053
It's gonna be 28,

05:34.053 --> 05:38.083
28 divided by h of two,

05:38.083 --> 05:39.462
if we're thinking of this as a sequence,

05:39.462 --> 05:42.388
divided by the previous
term in the sequence.

05:42.388 --> 05:43.990
So 28 divided by h of two,

05:43.990 --> 05:47.875
we know that h of two is equal to,

05:47.875 --> 05:49.215
is equal to two.

05:49.215 --> 05:50.050
We just figured that out.

05:50.050 --> 05:52.674
So we go back to 14,
something very interesting.

05:52.674 --> 05:54.440
I think you see where this is going.

05:54.440 --> 05:58.943
H of four is gonna be 28
divided by h of three,

05:58.943 --> 06:02.301
28 divided by h of three,

06:02.301 --> 06:04.251
which is 28 divided by,

06:04.251 --> 06:05.414
this is h of three right over here,

06:05.414 --> 06:06.386
we just figured that out,

06:06.386 --> 06:09.276
divided by 14, which is back to two.

06:09.276 --> 06:12.387
If we were to think of this as a sequence,

06:12.387 --> 06:16.033
we'd say, "All right, let's
see, the first term is 14,

06:16.033 --> 06:19.245
"then we get to two,

06:19.245 --> 06:21.055
"then we go to 14,

06:21.055 --> 06:22.235
"then we get to two."

06:22.235 --> 06:24.020
So one way to think about this sequence is

06:24.020 --> 06:27.317
that we just keep alternating
between 14 and twos.

06:27.317 --> 06:31.009
All of the odd terms
of the sequence are 14,

06:31.009 --> 06:32.890
all of the even terms
of the sequence are two.

06:32.890 --> 06:34.817
That's one way to think about it.

06:34.817 --> 06:36.328
Or another way to think about it is,

06:36.328 --> 06:38.346
we're starting with 14,

06:38.346 --> 06:42.015
and each successive term is
the previous term divided,

06:42.015 --> 06:45.126
is 28 divided by the previous term.

06:45.126 --> 06:48.284
So here, 28 divided by 14 is two.

06:48.284 --> 06:51.813
28 divided by two is 14.
28 divided by 14 is two.

06:51.813 --> 06:53.206
And we keep going on and on and on,

06:53.206 --> 06:57.223
and that's what was actually
going on right over there.

06:57.223 --> 06:58.582
Let's do one more of these.

06:59.592 --> 07:00.776
And this one is interesting,

07:00.776 --> 07:02.026
because we now have,

07:02.026 --> 07:06.636
we now have two base cases.

07:06.636 --> 07:08.397
So let's think about this.

07:08.397 --> 07:10.716
This is, and actually let's just say

07:10.716 --> 07:14.450
we wanted to figure out, we
wanted to figure out what,

07:14.450 --> 07:17.050
what f of four is.

07:17.050 --> 07:20.436
F of four, well, we're
gonna fall into this case.

07:20.436 --> 07:22.410
Four is greater than two
and it's a whole number.

07:22.410 --> 07:24.732
It's gonna be f of four minus two,

07:24.732 --> 07:29.440
so it's gonna be f of two
plus f of four minus one,

07:29.440 --> 07:31.460
plus f of three.

07:31.460 --> 07:32.830
So f of four is gonna be the sum

07:32.830 --> 07:35.338
of the preceding two numbers.

07:35.338 --> 07:36.360
All right, so let's figure out

07:36.360 --> 07:38.728
what f of three is going to be.

07:38.728 --> 07:40.725
F of three, we fall into this case again,

07:40.725 --> 07:43.987
it's gonna be f of three
minus two is f of one,

07:44.927 --> 07:48.642
plus f of three minus one, plus f of two,

07:48.642 --> 07:51.243
the sum of the preceding two numbers.

07:51.243 --> 07:53.727
So let's figure out what
f of two is going to be.

07:53.727 --> 07:54.772
Well, now we're not doing the sum

07:54.772 --> 07:56.212
of the preceding two numbers anymore.

07:56.212 --> 07:59.277
We fall into this base
case. N is now equal to two.

07:59.277 --> 08:01.274
It's going to be equal to negative four.

08:01.274 --> 08:02.435
And we're gonna have to figure out

08:02.435 --> 08:04.408
what f of one is as well.

08:04.408 --> 08:06.010
And we see when n is equal to one,

08:06.010 --> 08:07.891
f is equal to negative six.

08:07.891 --> 08:10.306
We have two base cases right over here.

08:10.306 --> 08:13.928
Base cases, cases that aren't defined

08:13.928 --> 08:15.344
in terms of the function itself.

08:15.344 --> 08:16.296
And you need that,

08:16.296 --> 08:18.061
'cause otherwise you'd
just be recursing forever.

08:18.061 --> 08:20.615
You would never get to actual numbers.

08:20.615 --> 08:23.502
But now we can use these to
fill in the values up here.

08:23.502 --> 08:26.954
So the sequence is negative six,

08:26.954 --> 08:29.833
then we go to negative
four as the second term,

08:29.833 --> 08:32.016
and then the third term is
the sum of the previous two.

08:32.016 --> 08:36.613
Negative six plus negative
four is negative 10.

08:36.613 --> 08:40.119
Negative six plus negative
four is negative 10.

08:40.119 --> 08:42.371
And then the fourth term is
the sum of the previous two.

08:42.371 --> 08:43.416
We see it right over here.

08:43.416 --> 08:45.854
The second term, f of
two, plus f of three.

08:45.854 --> 08:50.173
Negative four plus
negative 10 is negative 14.

08:50.173 --> 08:53.122
And we could keep going on
and on and on like that.

08:53.122 --> 08:55.815
So this right over here is negative 14.

08:55.815 --> 08:57.324
So the whole point of this video,

08:57.324 --> 08:59.809
you're a little bit familiar
with recursive functions now,

08:59.809 --> 09:01.573
and also you can see how these can be used

09:01.573 --> 09:04.638
to define actual sequences.
